  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1210 PM MDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe warming trend continues today and tomorrow as the Desert \nSouthwest remains trapped under a broad high pressure center. \nThough this airmass is drier compared to earlier in the week, we\nare still holding onto some mild mid-level moisture which will \npromote some scattered cloud cover and perhaps a few short-lived\nisolated showers. While this may help dull temperatures in a \nfew localized areas, most locations should expect high \ntemperatures to sit about 10 degrees above normal today, \ntomorrow, and beyond. This will push and potentially break high \ntemperature records through the weekend for many areas. As such,\nExtreme Heat Warnings and Heat Advisories remain in place for \nthe next several days.\n\nLate Monday and into Tuesday, a shallow trough transits the \nnorthern rockies with flow dipping into northern portions of the\nCWA. This should drop temperatures a few degrees across some \nnorthwestern Colorado areas, though not enough to be brought \nback to near-normal. This does bring stronger winds though,\nwhich could aggravate fire weather conditions, particularly in\nthe Yampa River Basin. Uncertainty remains on how far south \nthis flow will dip, particularly in regards to if it will reach \nthe Grand Valley and Utah Grand Flat where hot temperatures are \nthe most concerning. As of now, it is likely that these areas \nwill continue to hit triple digits through the entire week.\n\nLater in the week, the trough to our north pushes further into \nthe CWA, squashing the Desert Southwest high. This appears to \nallow some mild moisture to seep northwards towards the Four \nCorners, though PWAT's only rise to near-normal and is confined \nto the far southern reaches of the CWA. This could prompt some \nisolated showers along the San Juans, though as it stands right \nnow, impacts look minimal. It'll be worth keeping an eye on \nthough with this our next shot at any notable precipitation.",
